---

<Layout ogType="article" title={title} description={description}>
  <Header />
  <main class="pt-32 pb-20">
    <article class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8">
      <div class="mb-12">
        <p class="text-sm font-semibold text-brand-600 uppercase tracking-wider mb-3">Blog</p>
        <h1 class="text-3xl sm:text-4xl font-extrabold text-slate-900 tracking-tight leading-tight">
          Procore Pricing &amp; Cost 2026: What Subcontractors Pay
        </h1>
        <AuthorByline />
        <p class="mt-1 text-sm text-slate-400">
          Published August 10, 2026 · Updated September 25, 2026 · 9 min read
        </p>
      </div>

      <div
        class="prose prose-slate max-w-none prose-headings:font-semibold prose-headings:tracking-tight prose-a:text-brand-600"
      >
        <p>
          <strong>Is Procore free for subcontractors?</strong> For collaborating, yes: a sub invited by
          a GC works on that GC's Procore project at no cost, and can open a free company account to manage
          its team and bid invitations — but running your own jobs in Procore means paying for a license.
          <strong>How is Procore priced?</strong> By an upfront annual fee per product, scaled to your
          Annual Construction Volume (ACV), with unlimited users and no published price list.
        </p>
        <p>
          Because Procore quotes every deal, the numbers below come from contractors' reported
          quotes collected by independent pricing analyses — Projul, CostBench, and ScanManifold.
          Here is what those reports show, what a subcontractor actually pays (the free seat versus
          an owned license), and when the math works — so you know the territory before the sales
          call.
        </p>

        <div class="not-prose rounded-2xl border border-slate-200 bg-slate-50 p-6 my-8">
          <h2 class="mt-0 text-base font-semibold text-slate-900">Key takeaways</h2>
          <ul class="mb-0 mt-3 space-y-2 text-sm text-slate-600">
            <li>
              Collaborating on a GC's Procore project is <strong>free</strong>; so is a basic
              company account for your team and bid invitations (US and Canada).
            </li>
            <li>
              Owning Procore is a custom quote: an annual fee per product, based on
              <strong>Annual Construction Volume</strong> — not per user. Users are unlimited.
            </li>
            <li>
              Third-party analyses report about <strong>$700–$1,000 per $1M of volume</strong> and an
              entry point near <strong>$4,500/year</strong> (~$375/month). These are reported estimates,
              not a Procore price list.
            </li>
            <li>
              Reported ranges for GCs: $15,000–$30,000/yr at $10–50M ACV; $30,000–$80,000/yr at
              $50–200M.
            </li>
            <li>
              Budget beyond the subscription: add-on modules ($6,000+ each reported),
              implementation, and renewals reported at 5–14% higher.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <h2>Is Procore free for subcontractors?</h2>
        <p>
          Collaborating is free; running your business on Procore isn't. When a GC invites you into
          their Procore project, you use it on that project at no charge — for their RFIs, drawings,
          and submittals, with the GC deciding what you can see. Procore also offers a
          <strong>free Procore company account</strong> (currently US and Canada only) that lets you manage
          your team and business profile, respond to bid invitations, and reach every project where you're
          a collaborator.
        </p>
        <p>
          What neither gives you is Procore's paid project management or financial tools on
          <em>your own</em> work. Your bids, change orders, and
          <a href="/features/pay-applications/">AIA-style pay applications</a> across every GC still need
          a system you control — either an ACV-based Procore quote or a flat-priced tool like
          <a href="/pricing/">BuildWorkPro at $79/month</a>.
        </p>

        <h2>Does Procore charge per user?</h2>
        <p>
          No. Procore's pricing page is explicit: it charges "an upfront annual fee by product and
          based upon your Annual Construction Volume," and it will never charge you for adding users
          — including clients, subcontractors, and vendors. That is genuinely better than per-seat
          pricing for a large team. The trade-off is that the bill tracks your revenue instead of
          your headcount.
        </p>

        <h2>How much does Procore cost?</h2>
        <p>
          Procore does not publish a price: every quote is custom, based on your ACV and the
          products you license. Independent analyses report roughly <strong
            >$700–$1,000 per $1M</strong
          > of annual construction volume, an entry point near <strong>$4,500 per year</strong> for the
          smallest single-module operations, and <strong>$15,000–$30,000 per year</strong> for small GCs
          running $10–50M — before add-on modules and implementation. Treat every figure here as a reported
          estimate and verify with Procore.
        </p>

        <h3>How much does Procore cost per year for a small subcontractor?</h3>
        <p>
          There is no public answer, so work from the reports. Projul puts the starting price at
          about $375/month for the smallest operations. CostBench cites a median of $2,500/year
          across 19 verified purchases — a small sample, and likely weighted to single-product
          deals. At the reported $700–$1,000 per $1M, a sub running $3M of work computes to about
          $2,100–$3,000 a year, so in practice minimums and which products you pick tend to set the
          number. Each additional module is reported at $6,000+.
        </p>

        <h2>How the Procore pricing model works</h2>
        <p>
          Your quote is a function of two inputs: the annual dollar volume of construction you run
          through the platform, and which products you license (project management, financials,
          quality &amp; safety, and so on). Procore's copy is accurate — they really don't charge
          per user. But the structure has a consequence worth naming plainly:
          <strong>as your revenue grows, your software bill grows with it</strong>. Win $2M more
          work next year and the renewal quote knows.
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>Annual contracts.</strong> Projul reports contracts are annual at minimum, with many
            customers signing multi-year agreements for better rates.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Per-product pricing.</strong> Each product in the bundle is priced against your ACV;
            adding one later raises the bill.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Renewals.</strong> Projul reports increases of 5–14% at renewal, with 10%+ common.
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          That is the difference between "Procore cost this year" and "Procore cost over a
          three-year contract." A first-year number in the entry band can become a mid-size bill
          without you adding users — because volume and the product bundle moved, not your seat
          count. If a site lists a single "Procore cost" as if it were a SKU, that figure is
          someone's report of a quote, not a catalog price.
        </p>

        <h2>Procore plans: products, not a public rate card</h2>
        <p>
          Procore does not publish named pricing plans. The sales motion is: pick a product bundle,
          disclose your construction volume, get a quote. Public marketing groups the platform into
          product families, but those names are not a price list, and a 2026 quote may mix them
          differently than last year's.
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>Project management</strong> — the operations layer GCs buy Procore for: RFIs, submittals,
            drawings, and coordination at portfolio scale.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Financials</strong> — owner/GC money tools: commitments, invoices, and progress billing
            on <em>their</em> projects. That is not the same loop as a sub building an estimate, sending
            a
            <a href="/blog/aia-pay-application-guide/">pay application</a> against a schedule of values,
            and tracking retainage across every GC.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Quality &amp; safety</strong> — inspections, observations, and related field quality
            workflows. Whether they sit in your quote as core or add-on is a question for the rep.
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          Ask the rep to list what is in the quote versus what is an add-on. If you only need
          <a href="/features/construction-bidding/">construction bidding software</a> and
          <a href="/features/construction-crm/">construction CRM</a> for the jobs you sell, you are pricing
          a platform built to receive work from many trades, not to run your pipeline.
        </p>

        <h2>Reported Procore costs, by source</h2>
        <p>
          Annual figures below are approximate and independently reported, re-checked September
          2026. Verify current pricing with Procore; every quote is negotiated.
        </p>
        <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto rounded-2xl border border-slate-200 my-6">
          <table class="w-full text-sm">
            <thead>
              <tr class="bg-slate-50">
                <th class="text-left px-5 py-3 font-semibold text-slate-900">What</th>
                <th class="text-left px-5 py-3 font-semibold text-slate-900">Reported cost</th>
                <th class="text-left px-5 py-3 font-semibold text-slate-900">Reported by</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody class="divide-y divide-slate-100">
              <tr>
                <td class="px-5 py-3 font-medium text-slate-700">Entry point (single module)</td>
                <td class="px-5 py-3 text-slate-600">~$375/month · ~$4,500/yr</td>
                <td class="px-5 py-3 text-slate-500">Projul; CostBench low end</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td class="px-5 py-3 font-medium text-slate-700">Volume rate</td>
                <td class="px-5 py-3 text-slate-600">$700–$1,000 per $1M ACV</td>
                <td class="px-5 py-3 text-slate-500">CostBench; Projul contractor report</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td class="px-5 py-3 font-medium text-slate-700">Small GC ($10–50M ACV)</td>
                <td class="px-5 py-3 text-slate-600">$15,000–$30,000/yr</td>
                <td class="px-5 py-3 text-slate-500">ScanManifold</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td class="px-5 py-3 font-medium text-slate-700">Mid-size ($50–200M ACV)</td>
                <td class="px-5 py-3 text-slate-600">$30,000–$80,000/yr</td>
                <td class="px-5 py-3 text-slate-500">ScanManifold</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td class="px-5 py-3 font-medium text-slate-700">Additional module</td>
                <td class="px-5 py-3 text-slate-600">$6,000+ each</td>
                <td class="px-5 py-3 text-slate-500">CostBench</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td class="px-5 py-3 font-medium text-slate-700">Implementation</td>
                <td class="px-5 py-3 text-slate-600"
                  >$5,000–$15,000; $50,000–$150,000+ for larger rollouts</td
                >
                <td class="px-5 py-3 text-slate-500">CostBench; ScanManifold</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td class="px-5 py-3 font-medium text-slate-700">Renewal increase</td>
                <td class="px-5 py-3 text-slate-600">5–14% per year</td>
                <td class="px-5 py-3 text-slate-500">Projul</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p class="text-sm text-slate-500">
          $700–$1,000 per $1M works out to roughly 0.07–0.1% of ACV — the rate trackers use to
          sanity-check a quote, not a published Procore tariff.
        </p>

        <h2>What subcontractors actually pay</h2>
        <p>
          The free collaborator seat covered above doesn't run <em>your</em> business: your bids across
          every GC, your pay applications, your crew hours, your pipeline. Keeping your own system while
          collaborating in theirs is the normal setup, and our
          <a href="/compare/procore-alternative/">BuildWorkPro vs Procore comparison</a> covers how they
          coexist.
        </p>
        <p>
          Owning a Procore license is the other bill — the ACV quote in the table above. For a
          specialty sub whose customers are GCs, that license is easy to confuse with the free
          access you already have. The free access is the GC's project. The paid license is a
          platform priced for GCs and owners running portfolios. If your month ends with a pay
          application against a schedule of values, check whether the quoted products cover that
          loop — or whether you would still be stitching
          <a href="/blog/construction-change-order-management/">change orders</a> and retainage in a spreadsheet.
          That is the subcontractor angle on Procore cost: the sticker is only half of it; the other half
          is whether you are buying the right billing loop.
        </p>

        <h2>What Procore is actually worth it for</h2>
        <p>
          Fairness matters here: Procore is the deepest platform in construction for general
          contractors and owners running portfolios — RFIs and submittals at scale, drawing
          management, BIM coordination, a huge app marketplace. It also courts subcontractors
          directly, with a dedicated landing page for specialty firms. If you're a large mechanical
          contractor with a project-engineering department, the math can work. For smaller shops,
          the third-party read is cautious: Projul's analysis says Procore is probably not the right
          fit under $50M in annual volume, and one Capterra reviewer called it "a sledgehammer for
          smaller projects." Rollouts also take time — Projul describes weeks to months of setup.
        </p>

        <h2>Procore pricing vs BuildWorkPro</h2>
        <p>
          BuildWorkPro publishes its price:
          <a href="/pricing/">$79/month flat, or $790/year</a> — every feature, unlimited users, built
          for the sub-to-GC loop (bids, AIA-style pay applications, change orders, QuickBooks Online sync).
          There is no quote call because there is nothing to quote, and the 14-day trial needs no credit
          card. Against a reported Procore entry point near $4,500/year, the difference funds a crew member's
          tools, a truck payment, or simply stays margin.
        </p>
        <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto rounded-2xl border border-slate-200 my-6">
          <table class="w-full text-sm">
            <thead>
              <tr class="bg-slate-50">
                <th class="text-left px-5 py-3 font-semibold text-slate-900"></th>
                <th class="text-left px-5 py-3 font-semibold text-slate-900">Procore</th>
                <th class="text-left px-5 py-3 font-semibold text-slate-900">BuildWorkPro</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody class="divide-y divide-slate-100">
              <tr>
                <td class="px-5 py-3 font-medium text-slate-700">Pricing model</td>
                <td class="px-5 py-3 text-slate-600">Annual fee per product, by ACV</td>
                <td class="px-5 py-3 text-slate-600">Published flat rate</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td class="px-5 py-3 font-medium text-slate-700">Reported cost</td>
                <td class="px-5 py-3 text-slate-600">From ~$4,500/yr (third-party reports)</td>
                <td class="px-5 py-3 text-slate-600">$79/mo or $790/year</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td class="px-5 py-3 font-medium text-slate-700">Users</td>
                <td class="px-5 py-3 text-slate-600">Unlimited</td>
                <td class="px-5 py-3 text-slate-600">Unlimited</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td class="px-5 py-3 font-medium text-slate-700">Free option for subs</td>
                <td class="px-5 py-3 text-slate-600">Collaborator access + free company account</td>
                <td class="px-5 py-3 text-slate-600">14-day full trial</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td class="px-5 py-3 font-medium text-slate-700">How you buy</td>
                <td class="px-5 py-3 text-slate-600">Demo and sales quote</td>
                <td class="px-5 py-3 text-slate-600">Self-serve, no card</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td class="px-5 py-3 font-medium text-slate-700">Built for</td>
                <td class="px-5 py-3 text-slate-600">GCs &amp; owners</td>
                <td class="px-5 py-3 text-slate-600">Trade subs billing GCs</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td class="px-5 py-3 font-medium text-slate-700">Progress billing</td>
                <td class="px-5 py-3 text-slate-600">GC/owner financials on their jobs</td>
                <td class="px-5 py-3 text-slate-600">AIA-style pay apps, SOV, retainage</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td class="px-5 py-3 font-medium text-slate-700">Implementation</td>
                <td class="px-5 py-3 text-slate-600">$5,000–$150,000+ reported, by scope</td>
                <td class="px-5 py-3 text-slate-600">Self-serve setup with CSV imports</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p>
          Shopping the other quote-only platform? See
          <a href="/blog/buildertrend-pricing/">Buildertrend pricing and cost</a> — last-published plans,
          the renewal pattern, and the subcontractor fit question. Different buyer, same lesson: if the
          vendor will not print a price, budget the quote <em>and</em> the renewal.
        </p>

        <h2>Questions worth asking before you sign</h2>
        <p>
          A sales-led Procore pricing call is more useful if you arrive with a short list. None of
          these require you to accept the first number:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            What Annual Construction Volume did you use, and what is the renewal if volume stays
            flat versus grows?
          </li>
          <li>Which products are in this quote, and which are add-ons later?</li>
          <li>
            Is there an onboarding, implementation, or training fee on top of the subscription?
          </li>
          <li>Annual versus multi-year — and what happens if my needs change mid-term?</li>
          <li>Show me the full-account export (not item-by-item download) before I sign.</li>
          <li>
            If I am a sub already collaborating in GCs' Procore projects for free, which of these
            paid products will my team actually use?
          </li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <p class="mt-10 text-xs text-slate-400">
        Sources: procore.com/pricing; Procore support documentation on the free Procore company
        account; Projul, CostBench, and ScanManifold pricing analyses; Capterra reviews. Figures
        re-checked September 2026 — independently reported ranges are approximate; every Procore
        quote is negotiated. Verify current pricing with Procore.
      </p>

